"SWOT AnalysisSWOT AnalysisiYour VisioniA future in which every small professional firm can use automation and AI as safely and confidently as a large one. Ten years from now, Quillfield is a trusted name for practical automation among small firms across the north-east United States: a small team of automation specialists, a library of proven and well-documented workflows, and hundreds of client firms whose staff spend their days on skilled work. Success means clients who stay for years, recommend us to their peers and never feel locked in.Strengths1.Founder with hands-on automation and solutions architecture experience2.Three fixed-price services that are easy for a small firm to buy3.A Care Retainer that turns one-off projects into recurring revenue4.Low fixed costs: a coworking desk and no staffWeaknesses1.A single founder's capacity limits how many builds can run at once2.The plan relies on an SBA microloan that is not yet confirmed3.Cash runs short in mid 2027, even with the loan4.No case studies or client track record yetHow could these changes affect you?i12345TechnologyRules & lawThe economySociety & lifestyleSupply & deliveryEnvironment & climateOpportunities1.Small professional firms want to use AI but have no one in-house to set it up2.Automation platforms keep getting cheaper and more capable3.Referrals through accountants and professional associations4.Ongoing maintenance demand as firms add more toolsWhich of these fit you?iChanges in technology could help you.Changes in rules & law could help you.Threats1.Larger agencies packaging low-cost automation offers for small firms2.AI tools that let firms automate on their own, pushing prices down3.Stricter privacy and professional-conduct rules on the use of AI4.An economic slowdown delaying discretionary projectsWhich of these fit you?iChanges in the economy could hurt you.Back‹›Back
[fields] Founder with hands-on automation and solutions architecture experience | Three fixed-price services that are easy for a small firm to buy | A Care Retainer that turns one-off projects into recurring revenue | Low fixed costs: a coworking desk and no staff | A single founder's capacity limits how many builds can run at once | The plan relies on an SBA microloan that is not yet confirmed | Cash runs short in mid 2027, even with the loan | No case studies or client track record yet | Small professional firms want to use AI but have no one in-house to set it up | Automation platforms keep getting cheaper and more capable | Referrals through accountants and professional associations | Ongoing maintenance demand as firms add more tools | Larger agencies packaging low-cost automation offers for small firms | AI tools that let firms automate on their own, pushing prices down | Stricter privacy and professional-conduct rules on the use of AI | An economic slowdown delaying discretionary projects"
`;

